To construct a precise, mathematically sound bell curve using standard piecewise cubic Bézier segments, the critical setup happens in the transitions between the flat top and the asymptotic ends.
Instead of treating the curve as a single sweeping arc, a precise representation splits the curve at its mathematical anchor points: the inflection points. For a standard Gaussian distribution \(y = e^{-x^2}\), these inflection points occur exactly at \(x = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \approx \pm0.7071\) (or \(x = \pm\sigma\) in standard probability notation), where the height is exactly \(y = e^{-1/2} \approx 0.6065\).
Excluding the top cap and the long baseline tails, the core shape of the bell curve is defined by the shoulder segments that bridge these regions.

The Anatomy of the Transition (Shoulder) Segment

To map one half of the bell curve smoothly, the shoulder segment handles the steep slope of the curve. Because it must join the top cap and the tail with continuous velocity and acceleration (\(C^{2}\) or \(G^{2}\) continuity), its boundary conditions are tightly fixed.
For the right-hand shoulder (running from the inflection point down toward the baseline tail transition), the cubic Bézier curve requires 4 control points: \(S_0, S_1, S_2, S_3\).

1. The Anchors (On-Curve Nodes)

• Start Anchor (\(S_{0}\)): This is placed exactly at the inflection point.
	• Coordinates: \((x_0, y_0) = (0.7071, 0.6065)\)
• End Anchor (\(S_{3}\)): This marks the boundary where the shoulder transitions into the asymptotic tail. A standard geometric target for this boundary is \(x = 1.5\), where the curve flattens significantly.
	• Coordinates: \((x_3, y_3) = (1.5, 0.1054)\)

2. The Handles (Off-Curve Control Points)

The off-curve handles must align perfectly with the tangent slopes at the boundary points to maintain smooth transitions.
• Start Handle (\(S_{1}\)): This dictates the exit slope from the inflection point. The slope of a normal distribution at its inflection point is at its maximum absolute value: \(y' = -\sqrt{2}e^{-1/2} \approx -0.8578\).
	• To keep the curve smooth and prevent a kink at the joint, \(S_{1}\) must lie on the line:
\(y-0.6065=-0.8578(x-0.7071)\)
• End Handle (\(S_{2}\)): This dictates the entry slope into the tail segment. At \(x = 1.5\), the exact Gaussian slope has flattened out to \(y' = -3e^{-2.25} \approx -0.3162\).
	• To match the tail perfectly, \(S_{2}\) must lie on the line:
\(y-0.1054=-0.3162(x-1.5)\)

Exact Control Point Coordinates

When optimizing a cubic patch to minimize the deviation error from the true transcendental function \(y = e^{-x^2}\) across this shoulder interval, the ideal handle lengths partition the distance roughly into thirds along the x-axis:

